WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:22.980
Pleased to meet you! My name is Albert Dunbar and I am a 22-year-old actor living and working in London, England, in 1612.

2
00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:31.255
Some women who come to see me on stage say that I am the best-looking man in London, and I tend to agree.

3
00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:41.476
A good actor must pay attention to his appearance at all times, as well as to his speech and body movement, in order to get the best roles.

4
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:51.117
Of course I only appear in the most popular productions, especially the plays written by William Shakespeare, the greatest writer in all of England.

5
00:00:51.118 --> 00:00:58.260
My career began when I was only seven years old and the church organist in my little village heard me sing.

6
00:00:58.261 --> 00:01:05.814
He immediately told my father that I should audition for the children's choir at the Chapel Royal to sing for Queen Elizabeth.

7
00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:15.050
Excited, my father took me to St. James' Palace to audition, and afterward I was invited to be a member of the choir.

8
00:01:15.051 --> 00:01:23.723
It wasn't long before I was acting in plays for the queen too, since boys with high voices are needed to play all the female roles.

9
00:01:23.724 --> 00:01:27.967
No women are allowed on stage; it just isn't done.

10
00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:31.218
I had lots of fun wearing wigs and playing different parts,

11
00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:37.698
but at times there were tense moments when some of the plays poked fun at the people in the queen's court.

12
00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:43.873
The nobility—the lords and ladies—became very annoyed at the writers and actors.

13
00:01:43.874 --> 00:01:52.573
One time Queen Elizabeth became so angry that everyone in the show was briefly put in jail for offending her.

14
00:01:52.574 --> 00:02:01.766
When I was 13, Queen Elizabeth died, and I soon joined an acting company associated with the Globe Theater called the King's Men.

15
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:09.647
The company members said they liked my pretty looks and my sweet-sounding voice and hired me on as an apprentice.

16
00:02:09.648 --> 00:02:17.425
But most of all, they liked my ability to read since many young boys in England cannot read or write.

17
00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:21.990
There were other boys in the company, ranging in age from 10 to 15,

18
00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:28.795
and the older actors taught us how to speak loudly so any large audience would be able to hear us.

19
00:02:28.796 --> 00:02:37.519
We learned the galliard, a dance with quick steps and high jumps, and the canary, a very popular dance from Spain.

20
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.947
A music teacher taught us how to play the fiddle, lute, and recorder.

21
00:02:42.948 --> 00:02:52.255
Dancing and musicianship are both skills we need for our various roles since the audience always enjoys a lively song!

22
00:02:52.256 --> 00:03:00.246
But I must tell you, whenever I sing the sad song "Barbara Allen," the theater flows with tears.

23
00:03:00.247 --> 00:03:09.083
All the apprentices also learned how to fight with wooden swords for the fight scenes, which are the most exciting moments in the theater.

24
00:03:09.084 --> 00:03:13.598
I have come a long way from the choir, I can tell you!

25
00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:25.609
As a boy player, I was given spending money, which I used to buy treats such as oranges and gingerbread. I also bought books—I love poetry.

26
00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:33.072
However, we were fined if we were late, missed a rehearsal, or damaged our costumes.

27
00:03:33.073 --> 00:03:41.776
Costumes are the biggest expense for a theater company since they are basically the clothes that people wear on the very best of occasions.

28
00:03:41.777 --> 00:03:47.811
Staining a richly embroidered doublet or tearing a velvet cape is extremely serious;

29
00:03:47.812 --> 00:03:52.558
the Globe acting company spends hundreds of pounds yearly on costumes.

30
00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:59.062
Since we use very little scenery, elaborate costumes are really important.

31
00:03:59.063 --> 00:04:06.449
When a flag flies from the roof of the theater, people know that there will be a performance that afternoon.

32
00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:13.141
Now that I am a grown man and famous, an apprentice helps me dress backstage.

33
00:04:13.142 --> 00:04:22.707
Of course, since my voice changed, I no longer play women's parts; now I play the lead male roles, and I'm excellent at them.

34
00:04:22.708 --> 00:04:25.253
But even though I am a celebrity,

35
00:04:25.254 --> 00:04:32.690
I still can't help peeking at the audience through the curtain like a novice to see how many people have come to see our play.

36
00:04:32.691 --> 00:04:41.717
The groundlings are the poorer folk who must stand on the ground right in front of the stage. They pay a penny to get in.

37
00:04:41.718 --> 00:04:46.384
The wealthy pay more and have seats on the upper balconies of the theater.

38
00:04:46.385 --> 00:04:52.761
Vendors sell fruit and nuts, and young boys run back and forth delivering bottles of ale.

39
00:04:52.762 --> 00:04:58.062
People converse and some play card games while they wait for the show to begin.

40
00:04:58.063 --> 00:05:09.656
It is very noisy, but when the trumpet blasts three times, people know the play is about to start and they quiet down expectantly.

41
00:05:09.657 --> 00:05:20.867
Today we are performing Macbeth, a play with a Scottish king, a ghost, three witches, soldiers, and lots of blood.

42
00:05:20.868 --> 00:05:26.047
People are quite fond of it, and most actors play several parts.

43
00:05:26.048 --> 00:05:30.776
Of course I am playing the leading role of Macbeth.

44
00:05:30.777 --> 00:05:40.435
So when I arrived at the theater this morning, I paced backstage practicing my lines to make sure I had memorized them all.

45
00:05:40.436 --> 00:05:49.347
There is a man, called a book holder, who holds a copy of the play near the stage so he can whisper a line if an actor forgets.

46
00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:56.046
But I like to be totally prepared, and I've never needed any prompting.

47
00:05:56.047 --> 00:06:02.548
This morning the stage manager had a difficult time getting the throne in place for the actor who plays the king.

48
00:06:02.549 --> 00:06:09.149
He also had trouble making the trapdoor work perfectly so the ghost can disappear in act 2.

49
00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:17.372
Some apprentices bustled about before the play, filling pigs' bladders with blood so the sword-fighting scenes would look real.

50
00:06:17.373 --> 00:06:24.819
The actors hide the bladders under their arms and then break them open when another actor pretends to stab them.

51
00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:29.754
It looks very gory when the blood spills all over the place.

52
00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:35.760
It's a great trick, and audiences love it!

53
00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:44.176
Actors frequently perform on other stages around London, such as the Swan Theater, the Middle Temple Theater, and the Boar's Head Inn,

54
00:06:44.177 --> 00:06:47.678
which the owner renovated so plays could be put on there.

55
00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:54.717
We also travel all over the countryside to do shows in town squares and at local inns.

56
00:06:54.718 --> 00:07:01.387
Last year I was invited to the private estate of an earl to provide the entertainment after supper.

57
00:07:01.388 --> 00:07:03.853
But by the time I made my entrance,

58
00:07:03.854 --> 00:07:13.595
a few of the guests were fast asleep and snoring so loudly that no one could hear me sing my famous version of "Barbara Allen."

59
00:07:13.596 --> 00:07:22.745
I was paid well for my appearance, but I far prefer being at the Globe where my artistic nature is taken more seriously.

60
00:07:22.746 --> 00:07:24.900
Come see me when you are in London.

61
00:07:24.901 --> 00:07:32.398
Everyone knows me. Just ask for the great Albert Dunbar!
